  "worked_solution": "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+12y+8.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have that $f(-2)=(-2)^{3}+6(-2)^{2}+12(-2)+8=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have that $f(2)=(2)^{3}+6(2)^{2}+12(2)+8=64\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We know that $y=-2$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y+2)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y+2)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ | $6$ | $12$ | $8$ |\n|  | $\u2193$ | $-2$ | $-8$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y+2)({y}^{2}+4y+4) \\\\  & =(y+2)(y+2)^{2} \\\\  & =(y+2)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has exactly one equilibrium solution,\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
